This study focuses on key elements in cross-domain text-to-SQL parsing, specifically schema linking and value filling. To enhance encoding abilities, a column selection auxiliary task is proposed, leveraging explicit targets for relevance matching. Additionally, two value filling approaches are presented to bridge the gap between current zero-shot parsers and practical applications, considering that many existing parsers overlook value filling in synthesized SQL. Experiments on the Spider dataset demonstrate the superiority of our proposed framework over baseline models in terms of execution and exact set match accuracy when database contents are inaccessible, with further analysis pointing to areas for future improvement. The study's code is available on GitHub at https://github.com/sggao912/CDSQLS. 

Figure: Illustration of the main pipeline of our algorithm. Given a question $q$ and database $D$, our parser first generates a subset $\mathcal{C} \subseteq \mathbf{C}$ of columns $c$ relevant to $q$, based on a pre-trained model. Then, given $\mathcal{C}$ and question $q$, our parser determines a sequence $\mathbf{s}$ of SQL tokens. Finally, given $\mathcal{C}$, $\mathbf{s}$, and database $D$, our parser computes the desired target $\mathbf{t}$ in $D$. Note that in practical applications, $\mathbf{t}$ is obtained by applying $\mathcal{C}$ to $D$ via value filling. To facilitate value filling evaluation, our approach is to synthesize the required $\mathbf{s}$ and compute $\mathbf{t}$ from the given question $q$ and database $D$.\label{fig:CDSQLS_pipeline} \end{figure}

